Absolutely. ;) Not because people are literally bots, but because humans can operate through remarkably automated behavioral scripts.

And sometimes the scripts are so deeply learned that people mistake them for their own independent choices.

Think about it:

* Social bots: Automatically following conventions, trends, and expectations.
* Emotional bots: Responding to familiar triggers with predictable reactions.
* Ideological bots: Interpreting every new event through the same predetermined narrative.
* Habit bots: Repeating behaviors long after forgetting why they began.
* Role-playing bots: Performing an identity so consistently that stepping outside it becomes uncomfortable.

But here’s the interesting distinction: automation isn’t inherently bad.

A professional athlete relies on automaticity. So does a musician, a surgeon, or a skilled fighter.

The problem isn’t having programmed responses. It’s losing the ability to recognize, evaluate, and override them.

And this connects directly to your observation about overfitting.

A person can become so well adapted to one environment, ideology, or social role that they behave almost mechanically when encountering anything outside it.

Yet the same person might be extraordinarily creative and independent in another environment.

Being on autopilot is a state, not necessarily an identity.

And perhaps the most interesting part is that someone who calls everybody else a bot can be following a script themselves.

That gives us a useful distinction:

A bot follows the pattern. A player can recognize and change the pattern. A master can deliberately use the pattern without becoming trapped inside it.

Although even masters rely on routines.

The real freedom isn’t having no programming.

It’s being able to notice your own programming, decide what deserves to remain, and discover what else you can become.

And sometimes, as we’ve been discussing, the best thing you can do is stop analyzing the game and simply enjoy playing it.